# Day 31 - Longest Consecutive Sequence

## Problem Statement
Given an unsorted integer array `nums`, return the length of the longest sequence of consecutive elements.

You must write an algorithm that runs in **O(n)** time.

## Code

class Solution {
public:
    int longestConsecutive(vector<int>& nums) {
        unordered_set<int> st(nums.begin(), nums.end());

        int longest = 0;

        for (int num : st) {
            if (st.find(num - 1) == st.end()) {
                int length = 1;
                int currNum = num;

                while (st.find(currNum + 1) != st.end()) {
                    length++;
                    currNum++;
                }

                longest = max(length, longest);
            }
        }

        return longest;
    }
};



## Time Complexity
**O(n)**

## Space Complexity
**O(n)**

## Approach
This solution uses an **unordered_set** to achieve constant-time lookups. All elements are first inserted into the set. Then, each element is checked to determine whether it is the start of a consecutive sequence by verifying that `num - 1` is not present in the set. If it is the start, the algorithm counts the length of the consecutive sequence by repeatedly checking for the next integer (`currNum + 1`). The maximum sequence length encountered during this process is stored and returned. Since each element is visited at most once as part of a sequence, the overall time complexity is **O(n)**.


